From: Any OS
Subject: [PATCH] d3d11: SV_Coverage of target-independent rasterization

Problem: D3D11.1 target-independent rasterization (TIR) is a rasterizer
state with ForcedSampleCount N > 1 while a single-sample render target is
bound: the rasterizer samples N positions per pixel and the pixel shader's
SV_Coverage holds up to N bits.  DXMT honoured ForcedSampleCount only for
UAV-only rendering; with a render target bound the pipeline rasterized with
the attachment's one sample, so SV_Coverage was always 0x1.  Direct2D draws
non-realized path strokes with a translucent brush through TIR and scales
the brush alpha by popcount(SV_Coverage) / N, so such strokes came out at
1/16 of their alpha: the Windows 11 quick settings icons (light theme,
TextFillColorPrimary #E4000000) were drawn at alpha 0.056 instead of 0.894
(issue #1107; opaque brushes, fills, realizations and glyphs do not use TIR,
so the dark theme looked right).

Fix: a pipeline built while the bound rasterizer state forces N > 1 samples
and a single-sample render target is bound (MTL_GRAPHICS_PIPELINE_DESC::
ForcedCoverageMask) selects a pixel shader variant whose SV_Coverage reads
(1 << N) - 1 (SM50_SHADER_PSO_PIXEL_SHADER_DATA::forced_coverage_mask; still
ANDed with the blend state's sample mask).  RSSetState invalidates the render
pipeline when the forced sample count changes.  Other pipelines keep their
pixel shader variant, function name and disk cache entry: the mask is hashed
only when set.  The raster sample count stays the attachment's (Metal
requires the two to match), so fragments are still generated at pixel
centres only: TIR edges are not antialiased (stepped), but interior coverage
and alpha match D3D.
--- a/src/airconv/airconv_public.h
+++ b/src/airconv/airconv_public.h
@@ -284,6 +284,13 @@
    * value the geometry shader stored in the "SV_PrimitiveID" vertex
    * attribute instead. */
   bool gs_primitive_id;
+  /* AnyOS: target-independent rasterization (D3D11.1 ForcedSampleCount N > 1
+   * with a single-sample render target bound).  Metal rasterizes such a draw
+   * with the render target's one sample, so the input coverage
+   * (SV_Coverage) would hold one bit where D3D gives N; when nonzero this
+   * mask, (1 << N) - 1, stands in for the rasterizer's coverage (still
+   * ANDed with sample_mask).  0 keeps the rasterizer's coverage. */
+  uint32_t forced_coverage_mask;
 };
 
